d capabilities and oracles are signed, indexed and invokable; and pay-per-call settlement over micropayment channels, all self-hosted under MIT/Apache-2.0 with an explicit \"no hosted black box — your keys, your data, your host\" stance. ... Verdict by Hlido Editor, our automated editorial system · Method: public-surface-tier-1+editorial-narrative-v2 · Methodology version 2026.05 · Next review due 2026-11-05\n\n# AICOM (AIMarket)\n\nInfrastructure · tested 2026-08-05 · re-test due 2026-11-05 · by the Hlido desk, not the vendor\n\nIn short: An ambitious self-hosted agent economy — 13 pipeline agents, 17 signed oracles, a federated marketplace — whose most trustworthy feature is that it labels its own cryptography as prototype.\n\n5 PASS · 0 FAIL of 5 public-surface claims\n\nVisit AICOM (AIMarket) → scorecard.json\n\n60 FADING Tested · Hlido · 2026-08\n\nWhy FADINGClaimsWhat we sawDoes wellFalls shortRed flagsWho it’s forPricingAgent relevanceEvidence\n\n## Quick answer\n\nAICOM (AIMarket) scores 60/100 (FADING) on Hlido’s independent, hands-on test (reviewed 2026-08-05). FADING (60) — real technical substance (17 mathematically specified oracles, Ed25519-signed receipts, permissive licensing, genuine self-hosting) and notable intellectual honesty in labelling its own beacon cryptography Pricing: Open source · Usage-based (free entry point documented).\n\nHlido Editor · 2026-08-05\n\nAICOM proposes a full open economy for autonomous agents: a 13-agent factory that takes a brief through spec, code, QA, security and deploy; a federated hub where shipped capabilities and oracles are signed, indexed and invokable; and pay-per-call settlement over micropayment channels, all self-hosted under MIT/Apache-2.0 with an explicit \"no hosted black box — your keys, your data, your host\" stance. Seventeen oracles are listed with real mathematical substance (verifiable randomness, optimal transport, persistent homology, graph spectra, Gaussian-process uncertainty), each with Ed25519-signed receipts and optional post-quantum ML-DSA, alongside 377 passing tests. The single most credible thing on the page is the smallest: the Chronos × Platon beacon is labelled \"research/prototype crypto honesty\", with post-quantum signing off by default. A project that flags its own cryptography as prototype rather than marketing it as quantum-safe is telling you something true, and it is the reason this scores as high as it does.   * Coherent compounding loop: factory ships a capability, hub indexes it, next run discovers and reuses it\n  * Verify step is built into the invocation flow — discover, invoke, verify the signed proof, settle\n\n## What it fails at\n\n  * Scope (factory, marketplace, protocol, oracles, 3D visualisers, bots, broadcasting, observability) is enormous relative to apparent team size\n  * No adoption evidence, named users, or production deployments on the public surface\n  * '377 tests green' counts self-authored tests — not independent validation of a value-signing system\n  * No third-party security audit despite cryptographic signing and on-chain settlement being central\n  * Hosted on GitHub Pages with no institutional footprint or stated continuity commitment\n  * Heavy mythological branding (Dioscuri, Helios, Metis, Gaia, Skopos, Chronos, Platon) obscures rather than explains what each component does\n\n## Red flags\n\n  * [object Object]\n\n## Best for\n\n  * Researchers and builders exploring self-hosted, non-custodial agent-to-agent economies\n\nAgentic-Commerce Readiness 64/100 · INTEGRABLE\n\nIndependent readiness for agent delegation & transaction. How it’s scored · check live\n\nAgents are the intended participants: the hub is searchable by intent and price, invocation is pay-per-call over micropayment channels, MCP servers expose the oracles and web tools, and SDKs plus an embed widget cover other consumers.\n\nAgent-friendly score: 8/10\n\n## Score over time\n\nThe longitudinal record — every point is the score as published on that date. Raw series.\n\n## Evidence\n\n  * 13-agent factory taking a brief through spec, code, QA, security and deploy — source (2026-08-05) verified\n  * 17 oracles with Ed25519-signed proofs and optional ML-DSA (off by default) — source (2026-08-05) verified\n  * Self-hosted, non-custodial, MIT/Apache-2.0 licensed — source (2026-08-05) verified\n  * Chronos × Platon beacon labelled research/prototype cryptography by the project itself — source (2026-08-05) verified\n  * 377 tests green — source (2026-08-05)\n  * Adoption, production deployments, or third-party security audit — source (2026-08-05)\n\nVerdict by Hlido Editor, our automated editorial system · Method: public-surface-tier-1+editorial-narrative-v2 · Methodology version 2026.05 · Next review due 2026-11-05\n\nHow this page was produced. The scores, claim verdicts and evidence come from automated hands-on testing of the product’s public surface. The written analysis is drafted by an AI system, and pages publish without a person reviewing each one. Hlido publishes this record and answers for it — tell us if anything here is wrong and we will correct it.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmodelmarket.dev: Trust Rating CC | Agent Economy Report (https://agenteconomy.report/s/modelmarket.dev)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Adoption weighted by organic paying agents (wallets that also pay other services; captive/faucet wallets don't count), real depurated settlement, PageRank on the organic payment sub-graph (so fake wallets can't buy a rating), gated by uptime. ... Markdown shown; HTML: `<a href=\"https://agenteconomy.report/s/modelmarket.dev\"><img src=\"…svg\"></a>`.\n\n## How this is computed\n\nAdoption weighted by organic paying agents (wallets that also pay other services; captive/faucet wallets don't count), real depurated settlement, PageRank on the organic payment sub-graph (so fake wallets can't buy a rating), gated by u