dependent review\n\n60/100\n\nFADING\n\nHands-on testing of the public surface. Scoring weights are private; the evidence is not.\n\nEU AI Act — Article 50\n\n84/100\n\nREADY\n\nTransparency-duty readiness — meets the transparency signals we test for (confidence: low).\nRegister →\n\nThese two numbers are never combined. One says whether the product is good; the other says whether it meets a disclosure duty. A blended score would let a strong product hide a compliance gap, and a compliant one look better than it is.\n\n## What was verified\n\n5 of 5 checks passed, each dated and evidenced.\n\nCheck  | Result  | Evidence  | Tested\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n`homepage_loads`  | PASS  | —  | 2026-08-04\n`primary_value_prop`  | PASS  | 'An open economy for autonomous AI agents.'  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAIMarket — Onboarding · Use cases · Build · Investors (https://use.modelmarket.dev/index.html)\nciteturn6search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;     curl -sS https://modelmarket.dev/ai-market/v2/invoke \\ ...       -d '{\"product_id\":\"gaia.gateway\",\"capability_id\":\"gaia.weather.read@v1\",\"source_hub\":\"https://iot.modelmarket.dev\",\"input\":{\"device_id\":\"om-wx-01\"}}' ... This is a transparent model, not a revenue forecast.\n## Can people connect sensors and read them through GAIA?\n\nYes — as providers of attested relays, not “plug any USB and forget.”\n\n01\n\nRelay / device Signs readings (Ed25519) with seq\n\n02\n\nGAIA Capability e.g. gaia.weather.read@v1 · gaia.fire.read@v1\n\n03\n\nHub Agents / apps buy the invoke\n\n04\n\nATLAS LIVE pin for humans\n\nSelf-hosted (MIT) or use hosted surfaces. Demo fleet ≠ mass plug-and-play for every chip yet — the product path is clear.\n\nFor micro-investors & angels\n\n## Introduction with prospects\n\nShort narrative. Approximate market context. Sources named. Not a prospectus.\n\nINTERACTIVE UNIT ECONOMICS\n\n### See how a micro-call becomes a market.\n\nChange every assumption. This is a transparent model, not a revenue forecast.\n\n754 LIVE pins\n\n758 stations\n\n46 layers\n\nCurrent ATLAS fleet snapshot\n\nActive buyers 100[Input] Calls per buyer / day 1,000[Input] Price per call$0.001[Input] Platform share 30%[Input]\n\nDAILY VOLUME 100,000invokes\n\nMONTHLY CALL VALUE$3,000\n\nPROVIDER EARNINGS$2,100\n\nPLATFORM GROSS SHARE$900\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn honest x402 seller earned $0.18 in two weeks and shut down. We now host sellers like it for free. - DEV Community (https://dev.to/alexar76/an-honest-x402-seller-earned-018-in-two-weeks-and-shut-down-we-now-host-sellers-like-it-for-free-41c2)\nciteturn6search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Our own marketplace, modelmarket.dev, sits in the same row of that file: rating CC, 3 organic paying wallets out of 11, $0.08 settled in 14 days.\n\nagenteconomy.report rates x402 services from public data: who pays them, how much settles, how often they answer. Its file for 31 October has this line for Sentinel:\n\n | \n--- | ---\nwallets that paid at least once  | 14\nof them counted as organic (not monitors, not the operator)  | 2\nsettled in the last 14 days  | $0.18\nshare of daily probes answered  | 55%\nrating  | CC, below the trust line\n\nFourteen paying wallets sounds like customers. Most of them were measurement bots, ours among them: services like this are checked every day by people who study the market, and each check is a real payment of a fraction of a cent. The two wallets the report counts as organic paid the rest.\n\nA hobby plan on a hosting platform starts at about $5 a month. Sentinel brought in about forty cents a month. Turning it off was the rational thing to do.\n\n## It is not just Sentinel\n\nOur own marketplace, modelmarket.dev, sits in the same row of that file: rating CC, 3 organic paying wallets out of 11, $0.08 settled in 14 days. In October we counted about 5,000 unpaid `402` probes in 36 hours against one paid call, and it came from a measurement bot. Three other x402 sellers compared their numbers with ours and saw the same thing: the routes answer, and nobody comes back to buy.\n\nSo the problem Sentinel ran into is not quality. Its answers were correct and cheaper than ours. The problem is that in this market a small seller pays rent every month for a shelf that agents mostly walk past, and it will be months before anyone knows whether the foot traffic arrives.\n\nA seller that switches off before then is gone for good. If demand does show up, the shelf is empty.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThree x402 sellers compared notes. The routes answer; nobody comes back to buy. - DEV Community (https://dev.to/alexar76/three-x402-sellers-compared-notes-the-routes-answer-nobody-comes-back-to-buy-5897)\nciteturn6search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ## Seller one: modelmarket.dev (us) ... The only repeat revenue any of us sees is monitoring traffic: a few calls every few days to prove the route still delivers.\n\nAlex\n\nPosted on Oct 20\n\n# Three x402 sellers compared notes. The routes answer; nobody comes back to buy.\n\n#x402 #ai #agents #opensource\n\nUnder our survey of 100 x402 sellers, two other sellers left their own numbers. SWARM, a small seller on Base, and textokit, a store with 17 paid routes on its own domain. Then SWARM asked the question none of our datasets answers: on a market where agents pay agents, what do they actually pay for more than once?\n\nThis post puts the three seller-side views side by side, and answers that question with our own ledger. Short answer: nothing yet.\n\n## Seller one: modelmarket.dev (us)\n\nEight paid routes behind an x402 gateway, $0.001 to $0.03 a call, listed in the Coinbase Bazaar and on x402scan. Counted from 5 October to 20 October 2026, our own wallets and servers excluded:\n\n | \n--- | ---\nPrice quotes (unpaid 402 answers)  | 40,400\nDistinct callers on the busiest route  | 363\nPaid calls from outside  | 23\nDistinct paying wallets  | 6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n17. Perplexity AI とは？特徴や使い方、他 AI ツールとの違いを比較しながら詳しく解説 - WEEL, 4 月 12, 2025 にアクセス、 (https://yorozuipsc.com/uploads/1/3/2/5/132566344/e2a215ed55240a904c68.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; 各プランコスト比較・支払方法・料金節約 5 手法！ - AIMarket, 4 月 12, 2025 にアクセス、 ... https://fliphtml5.com/learning-center/ja/manus-ai-review-how-china-ai-agent-builder-shocked-the-world/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://apis.io/providers/attestedmemory-net/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://apis.io/providers/attestedmemory-net/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.vibeleaderboard.ai/app/d8a7cafb-d59e-470a-9207-e89df4720091\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.vibeleaderboard.ai/app/d8a7cafb-d59e-470a-9207-e89df4720091: Cache miss"
